1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,120
Four bottles, A to D, each hold one

2
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:05,000
unknown organic liquid.

3
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:07,200
Your job isn't to name them.

4
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:09,440
It's to say which functional groups your results

5
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:12,400
support, and which ones they can't rule out.

6
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:15,680
That takes three tests, the right controls beside

7
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:17,840
each one, and a safe plan for the

8
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:20,400
one reagent that contains chromium six.

9
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,720
The aim: to test unknown organic liquids for

10
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:27,520
a carbon to carbon double bond, a hydroxyl

11
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:29,840
group and a carboxylic acid group.

12
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:32,480
Your teacher tells you each unknown is a

13
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:36,000
hydrocarbon, an alcohol or a carboxylic acid.

14
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:39,200
Each unknown gets up to three tests, each

15
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:40,400
on a fresh portion.

16
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:42,800
You run the fizz test and the bromine

17
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:43,680
water test.

18
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:46,640
The dichromate test is a teacher demonstration.

19
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:49,840
The independent variable is which liquid is in

20
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:52,400
the tube, and the dependent variable is what

21
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:53,120
you see.

22
00:00:54,334 --> 00:00:57,613
Every test needs two controls beside the unknown.

23
00:00:57,854 --> 00:01:00,413
A blank uses distilled water instead of a

24
00:01:00,494 --> 00:01:00,973
sample.

25
00:01:01,374 --> 00:01:03,933
It should show no change, so if the

26
00:01:04,094 --> 00:01:07,093
blank does change, something else caused it: the

27
00:01:07,134 --> 00:01:09,613
tube, the water or the reagent.

28
00:01:09,934 --> 00:01:11,973
A known uses a liquid that has the

29
00:01:12,014 --> 00:01:14,653
group, like ethanoic acid for the fizz test.

30
00:01:15,054 --> 00:01:17,533
It shows the reagent works today, and what

31
00:01:17,614 --> 00:01:18,733
a positive looks like.

32
00:01:19,214 --> 00:01:23,053
So every row reads blank, known, unknown.

33
00:01:24,267 --> 00:01:26,906
You've already run the bromine water test, so

34
00:01:27,147 --> 00:01:29,426
use the same method, shown on the card.

35
00:01:29,707 --> 00:01:32,506
Cyclohexene is the known, and it fades within

36
00:01:32,667 --> 00:01:33,426
seconds.

37
00:01:33,707 --> 00:01:36,586
Water is the blank, diluted just as much,

38
00:01:36,627 --> 00:01:38,026
and it stays orange.

39
00:01:39,234 --> 00:01:41,513
Put two millilitres of the unknown in a

40
00:01:41,554 --> 00:01:42,433
test tube.

41
00:01:42,674 --> 00:01:45,713
With the gas-collection apparatus ready, add a small

42
00:01:45,794 --> 00:01:49,073
spatula of solid sodium hydrogen carbonate, the way

43
00:01:49,154 --> 00:01:50,113
your teacher shows.

44
00:01:50,674 --> 00:01:54,273
The known, ethanoic acid, fizzes straight away, and

45
00:01:54,314 --> 00:01:55,473
the blank doesn't.

46
00:01:56,114 --> 00:01:59,313
The gas bubbles through limewater, and carbon dioxide

47
00:01:59,394 --> 00:02:00,353
turns it milky.

48
00:02:00,994 --> 00:02:03,233
Judge a few bubbles against the known and

49
00:02:03,314 --> 00:02:03,953
the blank.

50
00:02:05,167 --> 00:02:06,846
Now the dichromate test.

51
00:02:07,247 --> 00:02:09,326
Your teacher runs it in a fume cupboard,

52
00:02:09,407 --> 00:02:13,086
with small volumes: two millilitres of acidified potassium

53
00:02:13,167 --> 00:02:15,966
dichromate in each tube, and five drops of

54
00:02:16,047 --> 00:02:16,766
liquid.

55
00:02:17,087 --> 00:02:18,846
The tubes stand in a water bath at

56
00:02:18,927 --> 00:02:21,806
about sixty degrees, with no flame nearby.

57
00:02:22,527 --> 00:02:25,966
This batch holds ethanol, the known, then unknowns

58
00:02:26,047 --> 00:02:28,766
B and D, and water, the blank.

59
00:02:29,487 --> 00:02:32,526
After about five minutes, ethanol and B have

60
00:02:32,647 --> 00:02:33,406
turned green.

61
00:02:33,887 --> 00:02:36,206
D and the blank stay orange.

62
00:02:37,434 --> 00:02:39,753
The teacher runs A the same way, with

63
00:02:39,834 --> 00:02:42,073
repeats of A and D, and they stay

64
00:02:42,154 --> 00:02:43,193
orange.

65
00:02:43,274 --> 00:02:46,713
C faded bromine water, and an alkene's dichromate

66
00:02:46,794 --> 00:02:50,313
result hasn't been checked, so C isn't tested.

67
00:02:50,514 --> 00:02:52,713
Green shows something was oxidised.

68
00:02:52,794 --> 00:02:55,273
There are no aldehydes among the unknowns, so

69
00:02:55,434 --> 00:02:58,473
green supports a primary or secondary alcohol.

70
00:02:58,674 --> 00:03:01,113
Orange means no primary or secondary alcohol was

71
00:03:01,194 --> 00:03:02,153
detected.

72
00:03:02,234 --> 00:03:04,233
It doesn't prove there's no O H group,

73
00:03:04,314 --> 00:03:06,793
because a tertiary alcohol stays orange too.

74
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,360
Record as you go, in a table drawn

75
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:11,360
up beforehand.

76
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:15,280
One row per tube: the liquid, the reagent,

77
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:17,840
what you saw, and only then, what it

78
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:18,480
means.

79
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:22,040
Write no visible change in full, never a

80
00:03:22,080 --> 00:03:25,040
blank cell, because a negative is evidence too.

81
00:03:25,600 --> 00:03:27,200
Record the controls as well.

82
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:29,840
If a blank changed, that row can't be

83
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:30,640
trusted.

84
00:03:31,867 --> 00:03:35,386
A fizzed, and its gas turned limewater milky,

85
00:03:35,467 --> 00:03:37,546
so a carboxylic acid is supported.

86
00:03:38,187 --> 00:03:40,746
C faded the bromine water, so a double

87
00:03:40,827 --> 00:03:43,146
bond is supported, but we can't tell about

88
00:03:43,227 --> 00:03:44,106
an O H group.

89
00:03:44,667 --> 00:03:47,946
B turned dichromate green, so a primary or

90
00:03:48,027 --> 00:03:49,706
secondary alcohol is supported.

91
00:03:50,187 --> 00:03:53,306
D changed nothing, so nothing was detected, but

92
00:03:53,387 --> 00:03:55,786
D could be an alkane or a tertiary

93
00:03:55,867 --> 00:03:56,346
alcohol.

94
00:03:56,827 --> 00:04:00,426
The teacher's key says D is 2-methylpropan-2-ol.

95
00:04:00,907 --> 00:04:03,226
Your claim still holds, because it said only

96
00:04:03,307 --> 00:04:04,746
what the tests could show.

97
00:04:05,967 --> 00:04:08,126
Now the three words from the skills video.

98
00:04:08,687 --> 00:04:09,166
Valid?

99
00:04:09,567 --> 00:04:11,486
Do the tests support the claim for these

100
00:04:11,567 --> 00:04:12,206
candidates?

101
00:04:12,687 --> 00:04:15,406
Fresh portions and clean controls rule out other

102
00:04:15,487 --> 00:04:18,606
causes, and the candidate list shows which reactions

103
00:04:18,687 --> 00:04:19,486
could interfere.

104
00:04:20,047 --> 00:04:20,766
Reliable?

105
00:04:21,167 --> 00:04:24,686
Repeat the deciding tests, like D's dichromate tube,

106
00:04:24,767 --> 00:04:26,206
and compare with another group.

107
00:04:26,607 --> 00:04:27,366
Accurate?

108
00:04:27,567 --> 00:04:30,126
Here the result is a claim about groups,

109
00:04:30,207 --> 00:04:32,046
so it's accurate when it matches the true

110
00:04:32,127 --> 00:04:32,766
structure.

111
00:04:33,247 --> 00:04:35,406
The knowns and the teacher's key show how

112
00:04:35,487 --> 00:04:36,366
close you got.

113
00:04:37,567 --> 00:04:38,846
Now the risks.

114
00:04:39,087 --> 00:04:43,566
Potassium dichromate contains chromium six: it's toxic, can

115
00:04:43,647 --> 00:04:46,206
cause cancer, and can trigger allergies.

116
00:04:46,767 --> 00:04:49,006
That's why your teacher handles it, in small

117
00:04:49,087 --> 00:04:51,566
volumes in a fume cupboard, with gloves and

118
00:04:51,647 --> 00:04:55,246
glasses, and chromium residues go into heavy-metal waste.

119
00:04:55,887 --> 00:04:58,286
The organic liquids are flammable, so use a

120
00:04:58,367 --> 00:05:00,446
water bath, never a flame.

121
00:05:01,007 --> 00:05:03,806
Bromine vapour irritates the eyes and lungs, so

122
00:05:03,887 --> 00:05:06,046
bromine water stays in the fume cupboard.

123
00:05:06,607 --> 00:05:08,926
Never smell an unknown to identify it.

124
00:05:09,567 --> 00:05:12,686
Organic residues go into the labelled organic waste,

125
00:05:12,767 --> 00:05:13,886
never the sink.

126
00:05:14,207 --> 00:05:15,646
Pause if you want to copy the full

127
00:05:15,727 --> 00:05:16,286
table.

128
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:21,260
One line for your claims: supported, not detected,

129
00:05:21,340 --> 00:05:21,980
can't tell.

130
00:05:22,620 --> 00:05:24,780
A positive with a clean blank supports a

131
00:05:24,860 --> 00:05:25,260
group.

132
00:05:25,660 --> 00:05:28,860
A negative means not detected, not absent.

133
00:05:29,340 --> 00:05:31,900
And when the tests can't decide, say you

134
00:05:31,980 --> 00:05:32,700
can't tell.

135
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:34,460
Your turn.

136
00:05:34,540 --> 00:05:37,900
In a demonstration, four test tubes were rinsed

137
00:05:37,980 --> 00:05:39,660
with ethanol to help them dry.

138
00:05:40,220 --> 00:05:44,060
Acidified dichromate was warmed with water, with ethanol,

139
00:05:44,180 --> 00:05:46,580
and with unknowns S and T.

140
00:05:46,940 --> 00:05:49,900
All four turned green, and a student concluded

141
00:05:49,980 --> 00:05:52,700
that S and T both contain a primary

142
00:05:52,860 --> 00:05:54,140
or secondary alcohol.

143
00:05:54,860 --> 00:05:58,020
Evaluate this conclusion, and propose an improvement.

144
00:05:58,300 --> 00:05:59,580
Pause here to answer.

145
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:10,880
Not valid.

146
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:14,080
The water blank turned green, so something other

147
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:17,360
than the samples reduced the dichromate: ethanol left

148
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:18,160
from the rinse.

149
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:21,120
So S and T's results can't be trusted.

150
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:24,080
Rinse clean tubes with distilled water and let

151
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:26,320
them dry, then repeat with a blank that

152
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:27,560
stays orange.

153
00:06:27,840 --> 00:06:30,320
Just repeating the test is the trap: the

154
00:06:30,440 --> 00:06:32,320
same ethanol turns it green again.

155
00:06:33,534 --> 00:06:34,173
Last one.

156
00:06:34,734 --> 00:06:38,573
Unknown E fizzes with sodium hydrogen carbonate, and

157
00:06:38,654 --> 00:06:40,733
the gas turns limewater milky.

158
00:06:41,214 --> 00:06:44,813
It decolourises bromine water within seconds, so it's

159
00:06:44,894 --> 00:06:46,733
kept out of the dichromate batch.

160
00:06:47,374 --> 00:06:49,613
Every blank shows no change.

161
00:06:49,934 --> 00:06:52,333
State which groups the results support, and what

162
00:06:52,414 --> 00:06:53,573
you can't tell.

163
00:06:53,854 --> 00:06:55,133
Pause here to answer.

164
00:07:05,534 --> 00:07:08,013
The fizz and the milky limewater support a

165
00:07:08,094 --> 00:07:09,533
carboxylic acid.

166
00:07:09,774 --> 00:07:12,173
The fading bromine water supports a double bond

167
00:07:12,254 --> 00:07:15,053
too, because one molecule can carry both.

168
00:07:15,294 --> 00:07:18,173
Without a dichromate result, you can't tell whether

169
00:07:18,254 --> 00:07:20,013
E has an O H group.

170
00:07:20,254 --> 00:07:22,733
Calling it an acid and dismissing the bromine

171
00:07:22,934 --> 00:07:24,173
result is the slip.

172
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:29,080
So, for any unknown organic liquid: three tests

173
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:31,720
on fresh portions, a blank and a known

174
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:35,840
beside each, observations before inferences, and only the

175
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:38,080
claims your results support.

176
00:07:38,680 --> 00:07:42,520
Next: mass spectrometry, which weighs the molecule itself.

177
00:07:43,734 --> 00:07:45,173
Here are the key notes.

178
00:07:45,414 --> 00:07:47,333
Pause the video if you need more time

179
00:07:47,414 --> 00:07:48,213
to copy them.
